---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const updated = "November 7, 2026";
const repo = "rt-labs-dev/rt-stream-releases";
---

<Base title="RT Stream privacy policy" description="How RT Stream handles your media library and your accounts.">
  <article
    class="max-w-2xl space-y-4 py-16 text-neutral-300 [&_a]:text-cyan-300 [&_a:hover]:underline [&_h2]:mt-10 [&_h2]:text-lg [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:text-neutral-100 [&_li]:ml-4 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:space-y-1.5 [&_ul]:marker:text-cyan-400"
  >
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-neutral-50">RT Stream privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] text-neutral-500">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <p>
      RT Stream is a media server you run on your own computer. This policy explains what data it handles, where that data
      goes, and how you control it. The short version: your media, your library and your accounts stay on your computer.
      To show posters and details, RT Stream looks titles up with public metadata services. We do not run servers that
      receive your data, and the app contains no analytics, advertising or tracking.
    </p>

    <h2>Data stored on your computer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Your library: the folders you add, the titles RT Stream recognizes in them, and the posters and details it
        downloads. These are stored in RT Stream's data folder, in a SQLite database and a folder of cached artwork.
      </li>
      <li>
        Accounts: the email address and password of each person you give an account, with passwords stored only as a
        secure hash. Two-step sign-in secrets are stored the same way when someone turns it on.
      </li>
      <li>Watch and listening progress, playlists and preferences for each account.</li>
      <li>Temporary streaming files while a video is being converted, which are removed afterwards.</li>
      <li>
        The data folder is <code class="text-neutral-100">%APPDATA%\rt-stream</code> on Windows,
        <code class="text-neutral-100">~/Library/Application Support/rt-stream</code> on macOS and
        <code class="text-neutral-100">~/.config/rt-stream</code> on Linux.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who can see your library</h2>
    <p>
      RT Stream serves your library to web browsers that can reach your computer, usually on your home network. Only people
      who sign in with an account you created can browse or play anything. Your media files are never uploaded anywhere.
    </p>

    <h2>Metadata services</h2>
    <p>
      To find posters, descriptions and other details, RT Stream sends lookups to these services. The lookups contain the
      names the app recognized in your files, never the files themselves. Like any website you visit, these services can
      see your IP address.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.themoviedb.org/">The Movie Database (TMDB)</a>: titles and years of movies and shows, and
        downloads of their artwork. See <a href="https://www.themoviedb.org/privacy-policy">TMDB's privacy policy</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://musicbrainz.org/">MusicBrainz</a> and the
        <a href="https://coverartarchive.org/">Cover Art Archive</a>: artist and album names, and album covers.
      </li>
      <li><a href="https://www.discogs.com/">Discogs</a>: artist details and images for artists found on MusicBrainz.</li>
      <li><a href="https://lrclib.net/">LRCLIB</a>: song, artist and album names, to show lyrics.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This product uses the TMDB API but is not endorsed or certified by TMDB.</p>

    <h2>Removing your data</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Delete a library in RT Stream to remove its titles and artwork. Your media files are not touched.</li>
      <li>Remove an account in RT Stream's user settings to delete it and its progress.</li>
      <li>Uninstalling RT Stream and deleting its data folder removes everything it stored.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      This site has no cookies, analytics or tracking. It is hosted on GitHub Pages, and installers are downloaded from
      GitHub. GitHub may log technical information such as IP addresses as part of operating those services, as described
      in{" "}
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes and contact</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the new version will be posted on this page with a new date. For questions or problems,
      open an issue at{" "}
      <a href={`https://github.com/${repo}/issues`}>github.com/{repo}</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
